<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="pipeline" title="The weaving pipeline">

    <show-structure for="chapter" depth="2"/>

    <tldr>
        <p>This is the weaving moment of <a href="weaving-model.topic">the weaving model</a> in
        detail, once a class the plan already named reaches the engine.</p>
    </tldr>

    <p>
        A driver hands bytes over and gets bytes back. Between the two stand four gates. Most
        classes stop at the first. Past the fourth, a class can still lose a merge or an
        injection without losing what already applied.
    </p>

    <code-block lang="mermaid">
flowchart TB
    A["Bytes from a driver"] --> B["The plan lookup"]
    B -->|no weave names it| X["Handed back unchanged"]
    B --> C["Class file format"]
    C -->|preview mismatch| X
    C --> D["Policy"]
    D -->|JDK, or the framework itself| X
    D --> E["The fingerprint"]
    E -->|this plan applied already| X
    E --> F["Injections, then merges"]
    F -->|nothing survived either half| X
    F --> G["Verification"]
    G -->|refused bytes| X
    G --> H["The stamp"]
    H --> I["Woven bytes go back"]
    </code-block>

    <chapter title="What stops a class early" id="early">
        <p>
            Four gates run before a single instruction is rewritten. This chapter covers three;
            the fourth, on the fingerprint, follows below.
        </p>

        <deflist type="full">
            <def title="The plan lookup">
                <p>
                    Two lookups by internal name. One finds the injections planned for this
                    class. The other finds the weaves that dissolve into it. Empty both times and
                    the class goes straight back. Nothing is reported. A driver that supplies
                    bytes lazily is never asked for them, unless an
                    <a href="extension-methods.topic">extension</a> is in force.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="The class file format">
                <p>
                    A preview class file loads only on the exact JVM that produced it.
                    <code>AW2004</code> fires when the weaver's own JVM is a different one.
                    Recompile the target against the JVM that will run it, or run the weaver on
                    the JVM that compiled it. An anonymous or local class only warns, as
                    <code>AW1092</code>, and is woven. The compiler numbered that name in source
                    order. Name the enclosing class instead, and narrow it with a
                    <a href="selectors.topic">selector</a>.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Policy">
                <p>
                    Asked once, and only for a class the two gates above passed. A JDK package is
                    <code>AW3001</code>. Reopen exactly the one you need in code: build the policy
                    with <code>DefaultWeavePolicy.Builder.allowPackage</code> and hand it to
                    <code>Weaver.builder().policy(...)</code>. It takes one package and never a
                    subtree. <code>java.*</code> is the exception, refused whatever you set, and
                    the framework's own classes are <code>AW3003</code> on the same terms. A class
                    file older than major version 50 is <code>AW2003</code>. Recompile the target;
                    its stack maps are absent or inferred.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>

        <note>
            <p>
                Two more of the standard policy's rules never fire here, because the engine
                describes the target from the class file alone. A signed artefact is refused
                only by a driver that knew beforehand. A declared weave class as a target is
                caught earlier still, at planning and again at compile time, never by this gate.
                <a href="policy-and-safety.topic">Weave policy</a> has the settings.
            </p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Already woven" id="idempotence">
        <p>
            Every plan carries a fingerprint. It is a SHA-256 over four things.
        </p>

        <list>
            <li><p>Its entries, in the order the plan holds them.</p></li>
            <li><p>The weaves that dissolve, and the members each of them declares.</p></li>
            <li><p>The plugins loaded, and the identifiers they registered.</p></li>
            <li><p>Each plugin's own metadata entries.</p></li>
        </list>

        <p>
            Where a weave was found on disk stays out. Two machines building the same sources
            therefore agree. The fingerprint is stamped into every class the plan named, and read
            back before that class is woven again. What happens then depends on the driver, and
            on whether the fingerprint still matches.
            <a href="choose-a-driver.topic">Choose a driver</a> has the three outcomes.
        </p>

        <note>
            <p>
                Dissolving weaves reach the digest in discovery order, unsorted. With two or
                more, another order gives another fingerprint, which the driver then treats as
                two plans meeting the same class.
            </p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Applying, whole or not at all" id="apply">
        <p>
            Injections run first, structural merges second, over what the injections produced.
            A declaration whose injector refuses is dropped and the rest still weave. A merge
            that refuses keeps whatever injections already applied. Only a refusal with nothing
            to keep hands your bytes back unchanged. The rewrite builds a fresh
            class rather than editing this one, so nothing half-woven is ever the result.
        </p>

        <list>
            <li><p>A declaration matching fewer positions than its <code>require</code> is
                <code>AW1043</code>. That is one of two origins for the code. The other is the
                point's own, reported when nothing of its kind was found at all; see
                <a href="injection-points.topic">Injection points</a>. Where the target
                legitimately varies, list the alternatives in an
                <a href="annotations.topic#weave"><code>@Group</code></a> rather than requiring each.</p></li>
            <li><p>More positions than its <code>allow</code> is <code>AW1044</code> — but only
                when <code>allow</code> is set to a bound. Left at the default of zero there is
                no upper limit, and the code cannot fire. Narrow it with an ordinal or a slice;
                <a href="injection-points.topic">Injection points</a> covers both.</p></li>
            <li><p>A target method grown past 65535 bytes of code is <code>AW4003</code>. Only the
                call costs anything here, not the handler's body. Cut injection points in that
                method.</p></li>
        </list>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Verification, and what a refusal costs" id="verify">
        <p>
            Woven bytes that reach this stage are examined twice, in order. Exception tables
            first, as <code>AW4004</code>: re-run with class dumps enabled and report the dump.
            Then the JDK's own verifier, as <code>AW4001</code>. Its remedy is to compare the
            <code>javap</code> output before and after; the first error names the method. Neither blames your
            target.
        </p>

        <p>
            The class writer itself can also report <code>AW4004</code>, for a class it refuses
            to write at all. That is a different stage from these two, with the same remedy.
        </p>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="STRICT">
                <p>
                    The default. A refusal throws, and weaving fails at the class it went wrong on.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="REPORT">
                <p>
                    A refusal is reported. The class you passed in comes back instead, and the
                    target goes on working unwoven. On the weaving path the run counts it as
                    failed rather than woven; a refusal from an extension's own pass touches no
                    counter.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="OFF">
                <p>
                    Nothing is examined, so the woven class is returned whatever it holds. An
                    unverifiable one fails when the JVM links it. The error names that class, and
                    nothing about weaving. <a href="configuration-keys.topic">Driver options</a> has the key that
                    sets this.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="The stamp a woven class carries" id="stamp">
        <p>
            The last stage writes two marks from one record, so they cannot disagree.
        </p>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="The AetherWeave attribute">
                <p>
                    Always written, for a class the plan named. It holds the weaver version, the
                    fingerprint, a flag word, and one entry per injection the plan named for this
                    class. Whether that injection survived validation does not matter. A merge
                    applied in the same pass is not among the entries. The policy-override and
                    structural flags are both written false.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="The @Woven annotation">
                <p>
                    As much as the detail level asks for. It alone carries the plugin
                    coordinates and metadata. <code>NONE</code> writes no annotation at all.
                    <code>SUMMARY</code> leaves out the per-declaration listing, and
                    <code>FULL</code> adds it, capped at 32 entries. A flag says when that listing
                    was cut. <a href="annotations.topic">The annotation reference</a> has the elements.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>

        <note>
            <p>
                A class the plan named nowhere is never stamped and never counted as woven. It
                reaches the verifier only when an <a href="extension-methods.topic">extension</a> rewrote
                something in it; otherwise nothing runs on it at all.
            </p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

